.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt BHYVECTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bhyvectl
.Nd control utility for bhyve instances
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar <vmname>
.Op Fl -create
.Op Fl -destroy
.Op Fl -get-stats
.Op Fl -get-vm-pid
.Op Fl -inject-nmi
.Op Fl -force-reset
.Op Fl -force-poweroff
.Op Fl -checkpoint= Ns Ar <file>
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar <path>
.Op Fl -suspend= Ns Ar <file>
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar vmname
.Bro Fl -migrate-fd= Ns Ar socket-fd |
.Fl -migrate-recover-fd= Ns Ar socket-fd Brc
.Fl -migrate-certificate-fd= Ns Ar certificate-fd
.Fl -migrate-key-fd= Ns Ar key-fd
.Fl -migrate-trust-fd= Ns Ar trust-fd
.Fl -migrate-peer-sha256= Ns Ar fingerprint
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar path
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar vmname
.Fl -migrate-fd= Ns Ar socket-fd
.Fl -migrate-plaintext-unix
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar path
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar vmname
.Fl -migrate-repair
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar path
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar vmname
.Fl -migrate-resolve= Ns Cm resume
.Fl -migrate-source-retired
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar path
.Nm
.Fl -vm= Ns Ar vmname
.Fl -migrate-resolve= Ns Cm discard
.Fl -migrate-acknowledge-loss
.Op Fl -rundir= Ns Ar path
.Nm
.Fl -migrate-journal= Ns Ar journal-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command is a control utility for active
.Xr bhyve 8
virtual machine instances.
.Pp
The user-facing options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "--checkpoint=<file>"
.It Fl -vm= Ns Ar <vmname>
Operate on the virtual machine
.Ar <vmname> .
.It Fl -create
Create the specified VM.
.It Fl -destroy
Destroy the specified VM.
.It Fl -get-stats
Retrieve statistics for the specified VM.
.It Fl -get-vm-pid
Print the process ID of the bhyve process running the specified VM.
In monitor mode, this is the child process executing the VM.
.It Fl -inject-nmi
Inject a non-maskable interrupt (NMI) into the VM.
.It Fl -force-reset
Force the VM to reset.
.It Fl -force-poweroff
Force the VM to power off.
.It Fl -checkpoint= Ns Ar <file>
Save a snapshot of a virtual machine.
The named file is an atomically published manifest.
It refers to generation-matched guest-memory, kernel/device-state, and
metadata files in the same directory.
Replacing a checkpoint leaves the previous generation selected until every
new member has been durably written.
A persistent sibling lock file serializes replacement with concurrent restore
and permits safe reclamation of the previous generation.
.It Fl -suspend= Ns Ar <file>
Save a snapshot of a virtual machine similar to
.Fl -checkpoint .
The virtual machine will terminate after the snapshot has been
saved.
.Nm
exits with status 0 once the snapshot is published and the virtual machine
destroyed;
.Xr bhyve 8
acknowledges the request before it exits.
If stopping the source fails after the snapshot has been published, the
.Xr bhyve 8
process exits with an error instead of resuming the guest; the saved snapshot
is the surviving copy.
.It Fl -migrate-fd= Ns Ar socket-fd
Initiate an experimental migration through the source bhyve process using an
inherited connected stream socket to a prepared destination receiver.
The command requires snapshot support and all four migration credential
options below, unless
.Fl -migrate-plaintext-unix
is given, and uses the source controller's canonical recovery directory.
It cannot be combined with recovery, VM actions, or query options.
It does not connect to a remote host or configure the destination.
Success requires a completed migration with the source no longer runnable.
An error or lost reply does not authorize resuming or recreating the source;
use the retained controller's recovery operation when retirement has begun.
.It Fl -migrate-recover-fd= Ns Ar socket-fd
Retry an experimental fresh-session migration through the retained source
bhyve process, using an inherited connected stream socket to the destination
recovery listener.
This operation uses IPC without opening the source kernel VM, which may
already have been destroyed.
It requires snapshot support and all four migration credential options below.
It cannot be combined with other VM actions or query options.
The source process must still own the original migration attempt.
Recovery does not resume the source or restore old tracing buffers.
Success requires confirmation that the destination acknowledged release and
the source remains retired.
.It Fl -migrate-repair
Retry the rollback of an experimental migration whose pre-COMMIT rollback
failed and left the source stopped and quarantined.
The source controller retries the same idempotent device, vCPU and console
resume sequence, with bounded backoff, and reopens ordinary control commands
only after it succeeds.
The controller refuses once source retirement has begun; a retired source is
never resumed.
The source already attempts this repair automatically after the failed
migration; this option is for a later operator retry.
It uses IPC without opening the kernel VM and accepts only
.Fl -vm
and
.Fl -rundir .
Success requires a repaired, runnable source.
.It Fl -migrate-resolve= Ns Cm resume | discard
Resolve an inbound migration that the destination bhyve process retained
because it failed after COMMIT may have reached the source.
The destination keeps the paused guest, which may be the only surviving
copy, and runs nothing until this command decides.
.Cm resume
runs the retained copy and requires
.Fl -migrate-source-retired ,
the operator's confirmation that the source will never run again.
The destination refuses it when an earlier resume attempt may already have
entered the guest.
.Cm discard
destroys the retained copy and requires
.Fl -migrate-acknowledge-loss .
The two confirmation options are specific to their action; giving the wrong
one, or neither, is a usage error and nothing is sent.
When the destination has a recovery directory, it journals the decision before
acting and the outcome afterwards; a journal that cannot take the decision
leaves the guest retained.
This operation uses IPC without opening the kernel VM and accepts only
.Fl -vm
and
.Fl -rundir .
.It Fl -migrate-journal= Ns Ar journal-file
Print a migration handoff journal, one line per entry, and exit.
The file is one attempt's journal from a recovery directory, named
.Pa h-s- Ns Ar challenge Ns Pa .log
for the source side or
.Pa h-d- Ns Ar challenge Ns Pa .log
for the destination side, as described in
.Xr bhyve 8 .
Each line gives the entry's position, side and kind: a phase transition, an
operator decision with the run witness that admitted it, or that decision's
outcome; followed by the destination VM identity and whether the migration
used TLS.
The journal is validated as its writer validates it; a damaged, truncated
or foreign journal is reported as an error and nothing is printed.
This option names no virtual machine, opens none, and accepts no other
option.
.It Fl -migrate-source-retired
Confirm, for
.Fl -migrate-resolve= Ns Cm resume ,
that the migration source is retired.
.It Fl -migrate-acknowledge-loss
Acknowledge, for
.Fl -migrate-resolve= Ns Cm discard ,
that the retained guest state is lost.
.It Fl -migrate-plaintext-unix
With
.Fl -migrate-fd ,
migrate without TLS over a local
.Dv AF_UNIX
stream socket whose peer is on the same host.
No credential options may be given, and the source refuses any other kind of
socket, as well as any virtual machine with HyperTrace enabled.
The destination must be started with
.Va migrate.plaintext_unix Ns = Ns Cm true .
Migration recovery always requires TLS.
.It Fl -migrate-certificate-fd= Ns Ar certificate-fd
Inherited descriptor for the source TLS certificate in PEM format.
.It Fl -migrate-key-fd= Ns Ar key-fd
Inherited descriptor for the source private key in PEM format.
The key must be in a private regular file owned by the source process user.
.It Fl -migrate-trust-fd= Ns Ar trust-fd
Inherited descriptor for the PEM trust bundle used to authenticate the peer.
.It Fl -migrate-peer-sha256= Ns Ar fingerprint
The destination leaf certificate DER SHA-256 fingerprint as 64 hexadecimal
digits.
For recovery, both authenticated certificates must match the original
migration attempt.
Neither the recovery directory nor the attempt identity can be replaced.
.It Fl -rundir= Ns Ar <path>
Specify path to the directory containing the bhyve IPC socket.
If not specified,
.Pa /var/run/bhyve
is used.
.El
.Pp
.Em Note :
Most
.Nm
flags are intended for querying and setting
the state of an active instance.
These commands are intended for development purposes,
and are not documented here.
A complete list can be obtained by executing
.Nm
without any arguments.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Destroy the VM called fbsd10:
.Pp
.Dl bhyvectl --vm=fbsd10 --destroy
.Pp
Running VMs will be visible in
.Pa /dev/vmm/ .
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
The snapshot file format is not yet stable and is subject to future changes.
Backwards compatibility support for the current snapshot file format is not
guaranteed when future changes are made.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bhyve 8 ,
.Xr bhyveload 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
command first appeared in
.Fx 10.1 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
The
.Nm
utility was written by
.An Peter Grehan
and
